{"relationshipsSections":[{"title":"Conforming Types","identifiers":["doc:\/\/GraphProtocols\/documentation\/GraphProtocols\/UndirectedView"],"type":"conformingTypes","kind":"relationships"}],"metadata":{"role":"symbol","navigatorTitle":[{"text":"Graph","kind":"identifier"}],"externalID":"s:14GraphProtocols0A0P","title":"Graph","roleHeading":"Protocol","fragments":[{"text":"protocol","kind":"keyword"},{"text":" ","kind":"text"},{"text":"Graph","kind":"identifier"}],"modules":[{"name":"GraphProtocols"}],"symbolKind":"protocol","remoteSource":{"fileName":"Graph.swift","url":"https:\/\/github.com\/Tyler-Keith-Thompson\/Grafluent\/blob\/main\/Sources\/GraphProtocols\/Graph.swift#L56"}},"abstract":[{"text":"An undirected graph: a finite set of vertices and a finite collection of undirected edges","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"between them. Kept apart from ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"DirectedGraph"},{"text":": an undirected graph is not a symmetric directed","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"one, and no type is both. ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"directed"},{"text":" views it as one when an algorithm needs arcs.","type":"text"}],"primaryContentSections":[{"kind":"declarations","declarations":[{"tokens":[{"kind":"keyword","text":"protocol"},{"kind":"text","text":" "},{"kind":"identifier","text":"Graph"},{"kind":"text","text":"<"},{"kind":"typeIdentifier","identifier":"doc:\/\/GraphProtocols\/documentation\/GraphProtocols\/Graph\/Vertex","preciseIdentifier":"s:14GraphProtocols0A0P6VertexQa","text":"Vertex"},{"kind":"text","text":">"}],"platforms":["Linux"],"languages":["swift"]}]},{"kind":"content","content":[{"type":"heading","text":"Overview","level":2,"anchor":"overview"},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"inlineContent":[{"text":"Edges and their identity.","type":"text"}],"type":"strong"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"edges"},{"type":"text","text":" lists every edge once (each copy of a parallel edge, and"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"each self-loop, once), and an edge’s position in "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"edges"},{"type":"text","text":" is its identity. The edge is reached"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"from both its endpoints through "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"incidentEdges(of:)"},{"type":"text","text":", always at that one position, so a value"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"keyed by "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"Edges.Index"},{"type":"text","text":" (a weight) is the same in both directions. Positions are valid until the"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"graph is mutated."}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"inlineContent":[{"text":"Counting.","type":"text"}],"type":"strong"},{"text":" Neighborhoods count edge ends. A vertex ","type":"text"},{"code":"w ≠ v","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" appears in ","type":"text"},{"code":"neighbors(of: v)","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"once per edge between them. A self-loop has both its ends at ","type":"text"},{"code":"v","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":", so ","type":"text"},{"code":"v","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" appears in","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"code":"neighbors(of: v)","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" twice per loop, and the loop’s position twice in ","type":"text"},{"code":"incidentEdges(of: v)","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"(Boost’s ","type":"text"},{"code":"adjacency_list","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":", LEMON, igraph). ","type":"text"},{"code":"degree(of:)","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" is the length of either, so a","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"self-loop counts 2 and the degrees sum to ","type":"text"},{"code":"2 * edgeCount","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":".","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"type":"strong","inlineContent":[{"text":"Vertex and edge indices.","type":"text"}]},{"text":" As for ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"DirectedGraph"},{"text":": with ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"vertexIndexBound"},{"text":",","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"vertexIndex(of:)"},{"text":" maps the vertices one-to-one onto ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"0..<vertexIndexBound"},{"text":", and","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"neighborIndices(ofIndex:)"},{"text":" gives adjacency in index space. A representation with dense edge","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"indices also reports ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"edgeIndexBound"},{"text":", and then ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"edgeIndex(of:)"},{"text":" maps edge positions","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"one-to-one onto ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"0..<edgeIndexBound"},{"text":" (Boost’s ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"edge_index"},{"text":", petgraph’s ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"EdgeIndexable"},{"text":"), so","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"algorithms that mark edges (bridges, cut vertices, Euler tours) keep that state in arrays too.","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"incidentEdgeIndices(ofIndex:)"},{"text":" lists them parallel to ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"neighborIndices(ofIndex:)"},{"text":".","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"type":"strong","inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"Laws."}]},{"type":"text","text":" Every conformer satisfies:"}]},{"type":"unorderedList","items":[{"content":[{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"code":"vertexCount == vertices.count","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":", the vertices are distinct, and every edge’s endpoints are","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"vertices; ","type":"text"},{"code":"edgeCount == edges.count","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":".","type":"text"}]}]},{"content":[{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"For every vertex "},{"code":"v","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":": "},{"code":"incidentEdges(of: v)","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":" lists the position of each edge with an end at"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"code":"v","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":" once per end (a self-loop twice); each such edge has "},{"code":"v","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":" as an endpoint;"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"code":"oppositeVertex(to: v, acrossEdgeAt: e)","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":" is "},{"code":"edges[e].oppositeVertex(to: v)","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":";"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"code":"neighbors(of: v)","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":" is the opposite vertices in the same order; "},{"code":"degree(of: v)","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":" is their"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"count."}]}]},{"content":[{"inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"The degrees sum to "},{"code":"2 * edgeCount","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":". "},{"code":"contains(edge:)","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":" is true exactly for the edges in"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"code":"edges","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":", in either orientation, and "},{"code":"contains","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":" is false (and does not trap) for anything"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"absent."}],"type":"paragraph"}]},{"content":[{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"text":"Asking again gives the same answer.","type":"text"}]}]},{"content":[{"inlineContent":[{"text":"With vertex indices, ","type":"text"},{"code":"vertices","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" is in index order: ","type":"text"},{"code":"vertex(atIndex: i)","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" is the ","type":"text"},{"code":"i","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":"th vertex.","type":"text"}],"type":"paragraph"}]},{"content":[{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"With vertex indices, "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"neighborIndices(ofIndex: vertexIndex(of: v))"},{"type":"text","text":" is "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"neighbors(of: v)"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"mapped through "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"vertexIndex(of:)"},{"type":"text","text":", in the same order."}]}]},{"content":[{"inlineContent":[{"text":"With vertex indices, ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"incidentEdges(ofIndex: vertexIndex(of: v))"},{"text":" is ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"incidentEdges(of: v)"},{"text":",","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"and ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"endpointIndices(ofEdgeAt: e)"},{"text":" is ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"edges[e]"},{"text":"’s ends mapped through ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"vertexIndex(of:)"},{"text":".","type":"text"}],"type":"paragraph"}]},{"content":[{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"With edge indices, "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"edgeIndexBound == edgeCount"},{"type":"text","text":", "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"edgeIndex(of:)"},{"type":"text","text":" is one-to-one onto"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"0..<edgeIndexBound"},{"type":"text","text":", and, with vertex indices too,"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"incidentEdgeIndices(ofIndex: vertexIndex(of: v))"},{"type":"text","text":" is "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"incidentEdges(of: v)"},{"type":"text","text":" mapped through"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"edgeIndex(of:)"},{"type":"text","text":", in the same order."}]}]},{"content":[{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"With edge indices, "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"edges"},{"type":"text","text":" is in index order: the "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"k"},{"type":"text","text":"th position of "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"edges"},{"type":"text","text":" has "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"edgeIndex"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"k"},{"type":"text","text":". So an algorithm that gathers edges from the index-space rows can order them by position"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"(a tie rule) without walking "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"edges"},{"type":"text","text":"."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"Defaults."}],"type":"strong"},{"text":" As for ","type":"text"},{"code":"DirectedGraph","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":", members with default implementations are requirements,","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"so a representation’s faster version is the one generic code calls, and a wrapper must forward","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"every one.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"text":"The protocol has no mutation; mutation belongs to concrete types. It does not refine","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"code":"Sendable","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":".","type":"text"}]}]}],"variants":[{"traits":[{"interfaceLanguage":"swift"}],"paths":["\/documentation\/graphprotocols\/graph"]}],"kind":"symbol","identifier":{"interfaceLanguage":"swift","url":"doc:\/\/GraphProtocols\/documentation\/GraphProtocols\/Graph"},"sections":[],"topicSections":[{"generated":true,"title":"Associated Types","identifiers":["doc:\/\/GraphProtocols\/documentation\/GraphProtocols\/Graph\/Edges-swift.associatedtype","doc:\/\/GraphProtocols\/documentation\/GraphProtocols\/Graph\/IncidentEdgeIndices","doc:\/\/GraphProtocols\/documentation\/GraphProtocols\/Graph\/IncidentEdges","doc:\/\/GraphProtocols\/documentation\/GraphProtocols\/Graph\/NeighborIndices","doc:\/\/GraphProtocols\/documentation\/GraphProtocols\/Graph\/Neighbors","doc:\/\/GraphProtocols\/documentation\/GraphProtocols\/Graph\/Vertex"